//! crypto/ed25519_decode.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the ninth rung: point decompression on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye,
//! composed entirely over the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye), recovering a full curve point from the 32-byte encoding that the group
//! law's compression produces. Ed25519 verification — the far side of the Lotus signed carry, Kumara identity, Vault, Comlink — receives a
//! public key A and a commitment R as 32 bytes each and must turn them back into points before it can check a signature; this file authors
//! exactly that inverse, decode, so compression and decompression now stand as a matched pair. It is the last piece of pure curve arithmetic
//! the verify equation needs; sign and verify themselves become compositions over this, the scalar ladder, and SHA-512.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, Monocypher-parity, audit-ready).
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-175524_rye-first-crypto-parity-and-the-decision-wave.md (Season G names Ed25519 as the Lotus
//! unblock) and the group law's own header, crypto/ed25519_group.rye, whose closing line names point decode as its remaining arithmetic rung.
//! Kin: crypto/ed25519_group.rye (compression, the inverse of this decode, GREEN) · crypto/ed25519_scalarmul.rye (the ladder, GREEN) ·
//! crypto/fe25519.rye (the base field, GREEN). Monocypher (vendor/monocypher, CC0/BSD-dual) is the parity TARGET, never a copied line — our
//! own composition, studied clean-room from the public mathematics of twisted Edwards curves and RFC 8032 (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE CRUX — a 32-byte encoding carries the whole y coordinate in its low 255 bits and the sign (low bit) of x in the top bit (RFC 8032
//! §5.1.2). To recover x from y (RFC 8032 §5.1.3): the curve equation gives x² = (y² − 1)/(d·y² + 1), so with u = y² − 1 and v = d·y² + 1 the
//! candidate root is x = (u·v)^((p−5)/8)·u, the standard division-free square-root form. Squaring back, v·x² is either u (the root is
//! right), or −u (multiply x by √−1 = 2^((p−1)/4)), or neither (the encoding names no curve point, and decode refuses). Finally, if the low
//! bit of x disagrees with the encoded sign, negate x. The curve constant d = −121665/121666 and √−1 are both derived in our own field
//! arithmetic, never copied literals. Nothing here is a claim about a device; it is arithmetic anyone can replay against RFC 8032.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device, no identity. Decoding a public encoding into a point is
//! arithmetic, not a secret and not a signature: this file holds no key and calls no std.crypto in the primitive. The exponentiations here
//! run a fixed ladder over public data (an encoding is public), so they are data-independent by construction; the whole-signature
//! constant-time posture stays the same named horizon the Season carries. Parity is proven at selftest against two references runnable now —
//! an algebraic identity anyone can check (√−1 squared is −1) and a round-trip through the GREEN compression (decode then compress returns
//! the original encoding) — and Zig's own std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.fromBytes as an independent implementation, matched byte-for-byte on
//! the recovered coordinates over the base point and its multiples, and matched in accept/reject verdict across a spread of encodings
//! including ones that name no curve point. Monocypher-source parity stays the same held-fetch horizon the other primitives carry.
//!
//! Run from the repository root:
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/ed25519_decode.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;
const fe = @import("fe25519.rye");
const pt = @import("ed25519_group.rye");

pub const ed25519_decode_version = "20260815.200500";

/// The one way decode can refuse: the 32-byte encoding names no point on edwards25519 (v·x² is neither u nor −u). A named error, surfaced
/// before any point is returned, exactly as verify-before-trust wants.
pub const DecodeError = error{NotOnCurve};

/// The field element 1, as a fresh constant (fe25519 keeps small constructors private, so decode builds its own).
fn fe_one() fe.Field {
    return fe.Field{ .l = .{ 1, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
}

/// Negate a field element — 0 − a.
fn fe_neg(a: fe.Field) fe.Field {
    return fe.sub(fe.Field{ .l = .{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 } }, a);
}

/// True when a field element is zero, decided on its canonical 32-byte encoding so two representatives of the same residue compare equal.
fn fe_is_zero(a: fe.Field) bool {
    const b = fe.to_bytes(a);
    var i: u32 = 0;
    var acc: u8 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) acc |= b[i];
    return acc == 0;
}

/// True when two field elements are equal, on canonical bytes.
fn fe_eq(a: fe.Field, b: fe.Field) bool {
    return fe_is_zero(fe.sub(a, b));
}

/// The curve constant d = −121665/121666 (mod p), computed in our own field arithmetic so no borrowed literal enters the tree. Decode needs
/// d itself (the group law needed 2d); one small numerator, one small denominator, both canonical before the inverse.
fn edwards_d() fe.Field {
    const num = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 121665, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
    const den = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 121666, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
    // invariant: both small constants fit one limb, so they are canonical field elements before inversion.
    assert(num.l[0] == 121665 and den.l[0] == 121666);
    return fe.mul(fe_neg(num), fe.invert(den));
}

/// Raise a field element to a fixed 256-bit little-endian exponent by square-and-multiply, most significant bit first. The two exponents
/// decode needs — (p−5)/8 for the square-root candidate and (p−1)/4 for √−1 — are both public constants of the field, so this ladder is a
/// fixed, data-independent sequence.
fn fe_pow(a: fe.Field, e: *const [32]u8) fe.Field {
    // invariant: the ladder walks a fixed 256 bits — the bound is a named width, not an open loop.
    var r = fe_one();
    var i: u32 = 256;
    while (i > 0) {
        i -= 1;
        // invariant: the byte index stays inside the 32-byte exponent and the bit index fits three bits.
        assert((i >> 3) < 32 and (i & 7) <= 7);
        r = fe.square(r);
        const bit = (e[i >> 3] >> @intCast(i & 7)) & 1;
        if (bit == 1) r = fe.mul(r, a);
    }
    return r;
}

/// The exponent (p − 5)/8 = 2^252 − 3, little-endian: the square-root candidate exponent. Derived by hand from p = 2^255 − 19 (bit 1 clear,
/// every other bit up to 251 set), so no borrowed limb pattern enters the tree.
fn exp_p58() [32]u8 {
    var e = [_]u8{0xff} ** 32;
    e[0] = 0xfd; // bit 1 cleared: …11111101
    var i: u32 = 31;
    while (i > 0) : (i -= 1) e[i] = 0xff;
    e[31] = 0x0f; // bits 248..251 set, 252..255 clear
    return e;
}

/// The exponent (p − 1)/4 = 2^253 − 5, little-endian: the exponent whose base-2 power is √−1. Derived by hand from p = 2^255 − 19.
fn exp_p14() [32]u8 {
    var e = [_]u8{0xff} ** 32;
    e[0] = 0xfb; // bit 2 cleared: …11111011
    e[31] = 0x1f; // bits 248..252 set, 253..255 clear
    return e;
}

/// √−1 in the field: 2^((p−1)/4) mod p, the square root of −1 the decode uses to pick the correct root branch. Derived, never a literal.
fn sqrt_m1() fe.Field {
    const two = fe.Field{ .l = .{ 2, 0, 0, 0, 0 } };
    return fe_pow(two, &exp_p14());
}

/// Decompress a 32-byte encoding (RFC 8032 §5.1.3) into a full extended-coordinate point, or refuse NotOnCurve. The one inverse of the group
/// law's compression: it reads y from the low 255 bits and the sign of x from the top bit, recovers x by the standard division-free square
/// root, corrects the root branch with √−1, and fixes the sign, returning an affine point (Z = 1, T = x·y).
pub fn decode(s: *const [32]u8) DecodeError!pt.Point {
    var yb = s.*;
    yb[31] &= 0x7f; // y lives in the low 255 bits
    const y = fe.from_bytes(&yb);
    const sign: u8 = (s[31] >> 7) & 1;
    // invariant: the recovered sign bit is a single bit.
    assert(sign <= 1);

    const y2 = fe.square(y);
    const u = fe.sub(y2, fe_one()); // y² − 1
    const v = fe.add(fe.mul(edwards_d(), y2), fe_one()); // d·y² + 1

    var x = fe.mul(fe_pow(fe.mul(u, v), &exp_p58()), u); // (u·v)^((p−5)/8) · u
    const vxx = fe.mul(fe.square(x), v);

    const has_m = fe_eq(vxx, u);
    const has_p = fe_eq(vxx, fe_neg(u));
    if (!has_m and !has_p) return error.NotOnCurve;
    if (!has_m) x = fe.mul(x, sqrt_m1()); // v·x² was −u: the other root

    const xb = fe.to_bytes(x);
    if ((xb[0] & 1) != sign) x = fe_neg(x); // make the low bit of x match the encoded sign

    const rx = x;
    const r = pt.Point{ .x = rx, .y = y, .z = fe_one(), .t = fe.mul(rx, y) };
    // invariant: the returned point is affine (Z = 1), so its coordinates read directly without a further inversion.
    assert(r.z.l[0] == 1);
    return r;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) {
        if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    }
    return true;
}

/// A deterministic spread of candidate encodings — the same generator shape the field's own selftest uses, seeded so each differs. The top
/// bit carries a varied sign so both sign branches are exercised; some seeds land on the curve and some do not, giving the accept/reject
/// verdict something to disagree on if decode were wrong.
fn test_bytes(seed: u8) [32]u8 {
    var s: [32]u8 = undefined;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < 32) : (i += 1) s[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% (@as(u32, seed) | 1) +% @as(u32, seed) +% 7));
    s[31] = (s[31] & 0x7f) | (@as(u8, seed & 1) << 7);
    return s;
}

/// Independent reference: Zig's own std.crypto edwards25519, used ONLY inside the selftest. The primitive above never calls it.
const ZEd = std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519;
const ZFe = std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.Fe;

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. Algebraic identity anyone can check: √−1 squared is −1.
    {
        if (!fe_eq(fe.square(sqrt_m1()), fe_neg(fe_one()))) return error.SqrtM1Mismatch;
    }

    // 2. Round-trip against the GREEN compression, and independent-coordinate parity against Zig's fromBytes, over the base point and several
    //    of its multiples — every one a genuine curve point, so decode must recover it exactly.
    {
        const b = ZEd.basePoint;
        const b2 = b.dbl();
        const zs = [_]ZEd{ b, b2, b.add(b2), b2.dbl(), b.add(b2.dbl()) };
        for (zs) |zp| {
            const e = zp.toBytes();
            const ours = try decode(&e);
            // decode then compress returns the original encoding — the two are a matched inverse pair.
            if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(ours), &e)) return error.RoundTripMismatch;

            // Independent-coordinate parity: our recovered affine x and y equal Zig's decoded affine x and y byte-for-byte.
            const zdec = try ZEd.fromBytes(e);
            const zi = zdec.z.invert();
            const zx = zdec.x.mul(zi).toBytes();
            const zy = zdec.y.mul(zi).toBytes();
            if (!bytes_eq(&fe.to_bytes(ours.x), &zx)) return error.CoordXMismatch;
            if (!bytes_eq(&fe.to_bytes(ours.y), &zy)) return error.CoordYMismatch;
        }
    }

    // 3. Accept/reject verdict parity: across a spread of encodings — some on the curve, some not — our decode accepts exactly the encodings
    //    Zig's fromBytes accepts, and when both accept the recovered points compress to the same bytes.
    {
        var seed: u8 = 1;
        var accepted: u32 = 0;
        var rejected: u32 = 0;
        while (seed <= 48) : (seed += 1) {
            const e = test_bytes(seed);
            if (ZEd.fromBytes(e)) |zp| {
                const ours = decode(&e) catch return error.ShouldHaveAccepted;
                if (!bytes_eq(&pt.to_bytes(ours), &zp.toBytes())) return error.VerdictCompressMismatch;
                accepted += 1;
            } else |_| {
                if (decode(&e)) |_| {
                    return error.ShouldHaveRejected;
                } else |_| {
                    rejected += 1;
                }
            }
        }
        // invariant: the spread genuinely exercised both verdicts — decode is not trivially accepting or rejecting everything.
        if (accepted == 0 or rejected == 0) return error.SpreadTooNarrow;
    }

    print("GREEN crypto-ed25519-decode: point decompression on edwards25519 authored in pure Rye — Season G's ninth rung, the inverse of " ++
        "the GREEN group law's compression (crypto/ed25519_group.rye), composed entirely over the GREEN base field (crypto/fe25519.rye). A " ++
        "32-byte encoding carries y in its low 255 bits and the sign of x in the top bit (RFC 8032 §5.1.2); decode recovers x from the curve " ++
        "equation x² = (y² − 1)/(d·y² + 1) by the standard division-free square root x = (u·v)^((p−5)/8)·u, corrects the root branch with " ++
        "√−1 = 2^((p−1)/4), fixes the sign, and refuses NotOnCurve when the encoding names no curve point. The curve constant " ++
        "d = −121665/121666 and √−1 are both derived in our own field arithmetic, never copied literals; the two exponents (p−5)/8 = " ++
        "2^252 − 3 and (p−1)/4 = 2^253 − 5 are derived by hand from p = 2^255 − 19. The primitive calls no std.crypto. Parity holds against " ++
        "two references runnable now: an algebraic identity (√−1 squared is −1) with a round-trip through the GREEN compression (decode then " ++
        "compress returns the original encoding), and our authored Rye equals Zig's independent std.crypto.ecc.Edwards25519.fromBytes " ++
        "byte-for-byte on the recovered coordinates over the base point and its multiples, and matches its accept/reject verdict across a " ++
        "spread of encodings that names both curve points and non-points. Monocypher's own decode passes the same mathematics — its " ++
        "vendored source is a held fetch, so Monocypher-source parity is an honest horizon rung. Purely local arithmetic — decoding a public " ++
        "encoding is not a secret and not a signature, so no real key, no signing, no network, no funds, no real device; Ed25519's signing " ++
        "with an identity key stays its own separate, gated round. Compression and decompression now stand as a matched pair, and Ed25519 " ++
        "verify becomes a composition over decode, the scalar ladder, and SHA-512.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
